[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: How a node changes state

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[NARRATIVE]
UID: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-LIFECYCLES
TITLE: How a node changes state
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
WIDGET: rows
STATEMENT: >>>
Raising DEPTH, accepting and superseding decisions, readiness, deletion and the migration.

- Who may raise DEPTH, and which steps are legal, is written nowhere. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM], [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-REQUIREMENT], [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK] and [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-EVIDENCE]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]. [GAP]
- A node that describes a flow — one of the operations the canon supports end to end, such as create, sign, check or ingest — reaches verified only when nix flake check carries a contract test for it plus a positive control, a case known to fail. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM] and [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK]. [WORKS]
- A decision goes from open to accepted by a human, in conversation, with no gate. The semantic model has to be able to express a transition that nothing checks. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]. [WORKS]
- Today that acceptance is written under the agent's key. Either the human writes the STATUS through scribe under their own key, or a semantic-layer rule has to allow an agent to write it on a human's behalf. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]. [GAP]
- A decision has never been rejected. Who may reject one, what a rejected or superseded decision does to the readiness of work depending on it, and whether that answer is the same for every node type, is unwritten — as is whether a dirty edge blocks readiness or is only reported alongside it. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. [GAP]
- When a decision's retirement condition fires it becomes superseded — done twice so far, by hand. There is no mark for 'expired', no transition, and no rule about who may do it. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. [GAP]
- An accepted decision's statement is frozen. Enforcing that needs the old value at the moment before scribe writes — a hook-layer capability that is not built. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. [GAP]
- No gate reads a probe's STATUS — one probe is unrun yet counted settled. Under the pipeline reading the field dissolves: 'run' simply means an evidence node exists. See [LINK: MECH-SPIKE-IS-A-PIPELINE-TO-A-DECISION] and [LINK: SPIKE-CONVERSION-SIGNAL]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-EVIDENCE] and [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK]. [FALLS OUT: MECH-SPIKE-IS-A-PIPELINE-TO-A-DECISION, run = an EVIDENCE node exists]
- Raising AUTHORED_BY from llm to llm-accepted or llm-adopted is a human act with no place to happen: no verb, no joint. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]. [GAP]
- 'Settled' means DEPTH at interface-settled or above, or a decision accepted. That predicate lives in a Python file today; under the layer stack it is a semantic-layer gate at the write and read joints. See [LINK: DEC-LAYER-STACK]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM] and [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-SEMANTICS-LIVE-IN-TOOLS-WITH-NO-OWNER]. [FALLS OUT: DEC-LAYER-STACK, an L3 gate at scribe.change pre and scribe.read]
- Does the readiness walk stop at a settled node, or does an open decision far upstream block everything downstream? Today one piece of work is verified yet reads as blocked. See [LINK: DEC-CLOSURE-STOPS-AT-SETTLED] and [LINK: SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK] and [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. [OPEN: DEC-CLOSURE-STOPS-AT-SETTLED]
- The ready query reports, for a piece of work: its own state, the frontier (unsettled dependencies whose own dependencies are settled), hop distance, degrees of freedom (open decisions in its closure — the questions), and dirty edges. It advises; it does not block. See [LINK: MECH-READY-QUERY]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK]. [WORKS]
- The walk itself stands; the table of what counts as settled is semantic-layer data and does not belong in the script. See [LINK: DEC-LAYER-STACK]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-SEMANTICS-LIVE-IN-TOOLS-WITH-NO-OWNER]. [FALLS OUT: DEC-LAYER-STACK]
- 'Supersede, never delete' is the rule for now. Expressed in the model it is 'no class may delete' — a handler refusing before scribe deletes. See [LINK: DEC-NO-DELETES-YET]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]. [OPEN: DEC-NO-DELETES-YET]
- Moving the canon onto the ruled types — three out, six in — is uncosted, has no work node, and waits on the plan-lifecycle decision. It must be done in one move, never half. See [LINK: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-REQUIREMENT], [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-USE-CASE], [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-EVIDENCE], [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK] and [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-NARRATIVE]. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-GRAMMAR-CANNOT-SPELL-THE-VOCABULARY] and [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-MEMBERSHIP-AND-COLLECTION-CONTRADICT]. [GAP]
- Four questions left over from the abandoned two-grammar sketch: a tier field versus nesting depth; whether 'ungroomed' is a tier or a type; whether RATIONALE stays optional on a decision; and a plan with no edge to its members. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. [UNADDRESSED]
<<<
